}}, {"kind": "story", "title": "\u0645\u0648 \u0643\u0644 \u0646\u062e\u064a\u0644 \u0646\u0641\u0633 \u0627\u0644\u0646\u0648\u0639", "body": "\u0646\u062e\u064a\u0644 \u0627\u0644\u062a\u0645\u0631 \u0645\u0648 \u0646\u0648\u0639 \u0648\u0627\u062d\u062f \u0628\u0633\u060c \u0641\u064a\u0647 \u0628\u064a\u0646 12 \u0625\u0644\u0649 19 \u0646\u0648\u0639 \u0628\u0631\u064a \u0645\u0646\u062a\u0634\u0631 \u062d\u0648\u0644 \u0627\u0644\u0639\u0627\u0644\u0645.", "punch": "", "claim_ids": ["c3"], "image_query": "wild date palm", "image": {"provider": "commons", "asset_id": "162914165", "title": "File:Date tree little.jpg", "description": "Wild and free just like a palm tree", "download_url": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Date_tree_little.jpg/1920px-Date_tree_little.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "original_url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d4/Date_tree_little.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original", "source_url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Date_tree_little.jpg", "credit": "E.Prabha", "credit_line": "Own work", "license": "CC0", "license_url": "http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en", "attribution_required": "false", "restrictions": "", "date_created": "2025-03-28 09:43:37", "width": 1600, "height": 2133, "licensing_verified": false, "sha256": "1b179d37ed73671094b6c3f186846bcba92ca0d382ded310b88114d30fcd54e8"}}]}, "feedback": "editorial_review_rejected: Card 2 ('\u0645\u0646 \u0648\u064a\u0646 \u0628\u062f\u0623\u062a \u0627\u0644\u0646\u062e\u0644\u0629\u061f') misstates the source: the Wikipedia passage says the date palm is native to the whole region 'from the Gulf States and Iraq to Pakistan and India' (a single native range), not that it originated in the Gulf/Iraq and later spread ('\u0627\u0645\u062a\u062f\u062a \u0632\u0631\u0627\u0639\u062a\u0647\u0627 \u0644\u062a\u0648\u0635\u0644') to Pakistan and India. This turns a description of native range into an invented spread narrative not supported by the cited text (c1), so the factual check fails. All other cards (info card on 6th-millennium BCE cultivation, spread across continents, palm longevity/height, date fruit size/sugar content, and 12-19 wild species) are accurately sourced from the quoted passage, images are relevant generic palm/date photography with no false historical staging, text is clean Saudi colloquial with no clipping, and the piece is a timeless local topic so timing is fine."}